Title: Chapter 3: What the Controllers Knew
Creation-Date: 2026-07-10
Last-Modified: 2026-10-04
Output-Formats:
Tagline: Working memory is the smallest tier, and every serious practice built around it: flight strips, why handwriting fixes memory in a way typing cannot, the abacus, taxi drivers' hippocampi, scratch paper, inner speech. Then the cost of using it, which is not the six thousand calories a famous myth claims but the opportunity cost of a tier that holds seven things, and the tier below it, where knowledge goes when it stops costing anything: instinct, reflex, trained reflex, and the groove the Greeks called hexis. Mentalizing runs on two rungs, not one, and the fast one cannot report that it is the wrong rung. The model has grown both halves, the pool and the page; then Ataraxos installs both on purpose in the hardest hidden-information setting there is and beats the most decorated Stratego player in history in one week on sixteen GPUs for under eight thousand dollars, which is this book's claim with a number attached. And the field has started building the reflex tier on purpose.
